>      # (one of 0.0, 0.5 or 1.0 for each individual at this SNP)
>      mu[i] <- inprod(fracs[1:10], ratio[i,1:10]);
>
>      # model the observed number of allele A reads as a binomial
>      # distribution with rate parameter governed by fractions x genotypes
>      snpA[i] ~ dbin(mu[i], total[i]);
>    }
> }
>
>
> would be written in Stan notation as:
>
> model {
>     fracs ~ dirichlet(alpha);
>     snpA ~ binomial(total, ratio * fracs);
> }
>
> This is assuming you define the types of data and
> parameters as:
>
>    simplex[10] fracs;
>    vector<lower=0>[10] alpha;
>    matrix[N,10] ratio;
>
>    integer<lower=0>[N] total;
>    integer<lower=0>[N] snpA;
>
> snpA and total have to be data because Stan doesn't support
> integer parameters.  Any of the other values can be parameters
> (or transformed parameters).
>
> If alpha=1, you can drop the fracs sampling statement because
> the implicit distribution is uniform.  If you want to keep it
> like this, you could define alpha to be rep_vector(1,10) in
> the transformed data block.
>
