messages.body_plain:
The main problem you are having with you model is the link between the linear model for mu, and Stan's parameterization of the Gamma-Poisson, aka, neg binom.
You can parameterize the linear model for mu however you want, and using matrix algebra will be faster as you point out. But this isn't the main issue, the main issue is relating the model of mean to the Gamma distribution parameters A[n] and B.

Below is a little more fleshed out, and the ordering is corrected.

Theta ~ normal(0,10);
B ~ gamma(10,0.01);

Mu[n]<- Theta[1] + Theta[2]*X1[n] + Theta[3]*X2[n];          \\+ etc.. this can be done using matrices instead...

for( n in 1:N){                                              \\ this is what will link the model of the mean to the parametrization of the neg binom
A[n]<-exp(Mu[n])*B; }
}

for( n in 1:N){
Y[n] ~ negative_binomial(A[n],B);
}

